# ==========================================================================
#  O08  A concrete kind and a role: Person and Author
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    Person is a rigid kind and Author is a role a person plays, so Author
#    is placed under Person and never the other way round.
#
#  WHY
#    Ask of a class: could one of its instances stop being one and still
#    exist? Rhona Blackwood cannot stop being a person; if she is not a
#    person she is not anything. Person is rigid, written +R. She could
#    have stopped being an author, by never writing the second book, or
#    never the first; she would still be Rhona Blackwood. Author is anti-
#    rigid, written ~R: every instance of it could, in principle, fail to
#    be one. Guarino and Welty call the first kind of class a type and the
#    second a role, and the distinction is the most useful single thing
#    OntoClean offers.
#
#    A role has two more marks. It is anti-rigid, and it is dependent,
#    written +D: nobody is an author except by way of a work. Author exists
#    only in relation to Work, in the way that Customer exists only in
#    relation to a sale and Employee only in relation to an employer. The
#    Trail's own definition says so: 'someone who wrote at least one work
#    in this dataset'.
#
#    The consequence for the hierarchy is a rule, not a preference. An
#    anti-rigid class may sit under a rigid one, because a role is played
#    by things of some kind: Author under Person is fine. A rigid class may
#    never sit under an anti-rigid one, because every person would then be
#    an author by necessity, which contradicts the role being optional. o09
#    shows what that looks like when it goes wrong. The set picture is the
#    same: the set of authors is a subset of the set of persons, and which
#    subset it is changes as books are written; the set of persons does not
#    change because of it.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#                         bs:Person   +R   rigid: an instance cannot stop being one
#                            |             (Rhona is a person in every circumstance)
#                            |  rdfs:subClassOf   allowed: anti-rigid under rigid
#                            |
#                         bs:Author   ~R   anti-rigid: every instance could stop
#                                      +D   dependent: on some Work
#
#       the set of authors is a subset of the set of persons, and
#       which subset it is depends on what has been written. The set
#       of persons does not depend on the books.
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - Rigidity is the first question to ask of a class: could an
#      instance stop being one and still exist? +R if never, ~R if always
#      possible.
#    - A role is anti-rigid and dependent. Author, Customer, Employee,
#      Patient, Member: things of some kind, in a relation, for a while.
#    - A role goes under the kind that plays it. Never put a kind under a
#      role.
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  OPEN IN THE EDITOR
#            https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F02-systematics%2Fo08-a-concrete-kind-and-a-role-person-and-author.ttl
#  SOURCES   Guarino and Welty, Evaluating ontological decisions with OntoClean, CACM 45(2), 2002
#            https://doi.org/10.1145/503124.503150
#            OntoClean: the four meta-properties
#            https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OntoClean
#  MEASURED  parses, 34 triples; 3 classes, 1 individuals; OWL 2 DL in profile; HermiT: consistent, nothing new; 'The rigidity of every class' 3 rows
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/> .
@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix oc:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/ontoclean#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

# Borrowed annotation properties, declared so the file is OWL 2 DL on its own.
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .
rdfs:isDefinedBy    a owl:AnnotationProperty .

oc:rigidity    a owl:AnnotationProperty ; rdfs:label "rigidity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "+R rigid: no instance can stop being one. ~R anti-rigid: every instance could stop. -R neither."@en .
oc:identity    a owl:AnnotationProperty ; rdfs:label "identity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "+I carries a criterion of identity; +O supplies its own; -I carries none."@en .
oc:unity       a owl:AnnotationProperty ; rdfs:label "unity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "+U every instance is a whole under one relation; ~U no instance is; -U neither."@en .
oc:dependence  a owl:AnnotationProperty ; rdfs:label "dependence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "+D an instance exists only if some other thing does; -D it does not."@en .

bs:Person
    a              owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label     "Person"@en ;
    rdfs:comment   "A human being."@en ;
    oc:rigidity    "+R" ;
    oc:identity    "+O" ;
    oc:dependence  "-D" .

bs:Author
    a                owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf  bs:Person ;
    rdfs:label       "Author"@en ;
    rdfs:comment     "Someone who wrote at least one work. A role: played by a person, for as long as the works exist."@en ;
    oc:rigidity      "~R" ;
    oc:identity      "+I" ;
    oc:dependence    "+D" .

bs:Work
    a              owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label     "Work"@en ;
    oc:rigidity    "+R" ;
    oc:identity    "+O" .

bt:author-rhona-blackwood
    a           bs:Author, bs:Person ;
    rdfs:label  "Rhona Blackwood"@en .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- The rigidity of every class  (on the model)
#  PREFIX oc: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/ontoclean#>
#  PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
#  SELECT ?class ?rigidity ?under WHERE {
#    ?class oc:rigidity ?rigidity .
#    OPTIONAL { ?class rdfs:subClassOf ?under }
#  } ORDER BY ?class
